# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ward General App - Ward General application configuration
#
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
#   Copy it to wardgeneral.env, then fill in the values below.
#
#       cp wardgeneral.env.template wardgeneral.env      # macOS / Linux
#       Copy-Item wardgeneral.env.template wardgeneral.env   # PowerShell
#
#   Or just run ./load-env.ps1 once -- if wardgeneral.env is missing it makes it from
#   this template and tells you to fill it in.
#
#   wardgeneral.env is git-ignored. Your server name never reaches a commit, and a
#   `git pull` never collides with your edits. This template, with nothing but
#   placeholders in it, is the part that ships.
#
# WHY THERE ARE NO DEFAULTS
#   Nothing here is pre-filled, because a default resource name is always
#   somebody else's environment. A value still in <angle brackets> counts as
#   unfilled, and the scripts stop rather than run against the wrong server.
#
# THIS FILE STANDS ON ITS OWN
#   You can copy these two values out of your Getting Started notes, but nothing in
#   Ward General App reaches into another area to find them. Each area's file is
#   complete by itself, so the areas can be done in any order, months apart.
#
# THIS FILE IS OPTIONAL FOR THE WARD GENERAL APP
#   Ward General App has three ways to point the app at your database, and this is only
#   the most convenient one. If you would rather use
#   src/WardGeneral.Web/appsettings.Development.json, or pass
#   ./run.ps1 -Server <your-server>.database.windows.net, those still work and
#   this file can stay uncreated. AI-Ready SQL leans on its file much harder,
#   because it has far more to configure.
#
# THERE ARE NO SECRETS HERE
#   The app signs in with your own Microsoft Entra identity through
#   DefaultAzureCredential. There is no password and no connection string to
#   store -- only the name of the server to talk to.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------


# The FULL host name of your logical server, including .database.windows.net.
# This is the one place in the project that wants the FQDN rather than the short
# name -- it goes into a connection string, not an `az` command.
#   right: contoso-sql-01.database.windows.net
#   wrong: contoso-sql-01
WardGeneral__Server=<your-sql-server>.database.windows.net

# The database Getting Started created. Leave this alone unless you renamed it.
WardGeneral__Database=wardgeneral
